WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.672
Narrator: In a lot of the
behind-the-scenes shots

00:00:02.672 --> 00:00:06.837
from "Avatar: The Way of Water,"
you'll see these monitors.

00:00:06.837 --> 00:00:08.760
Zoom in and you'll see the performance

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.027
of the actor playing
Quaritch, a 9-foot-tall Na'vi.

00:00:13.027 --> 00:00:15.360
The robotic monitor was a reference

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.070
for the actor in the scene

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.137
and for the VFX team who
added the CG giant in post.

00:00:21.137 --> 00:00:24.330
It was a huge step forward
from the first "Avatar,"

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.212
which only had a handful of scenes

00:00:26.212 --> 00:00:30.245
with humans and Na'vi interacting
compared to the sequel.

00:00:30.245 --> 00:00:32.010
Filmmakers have been making actors

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.470
taller or smaller than they really are

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.000
for over a century.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.490
But making sure the scale is realistic

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.440
without ruining the illusion

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.900
is where the real work begins.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.600
And one of the pioneers
in the space is Wētā,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.860
the visual effects company

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.170
that's overseen some of
the most groundbreaking

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.600
CG characters of all sizes,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.370
in movies like "The Lord of the Rings"

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.750
and "The Way of Water."

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.610
Eric: We always have to play games in film

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.200
to make that feel

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.750
and actually be able to
get good performances

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.940
from the different scale that you want.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.482
If they're 6-foot, the
tree's going to need to be

00:01:04.482 --> 00:01:07.140
30% smaller if the characters
are going to be 30% bigger.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.270
So there's a lot of
planning involved there.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.554
Narrator: It's a puzzle filmmakers
have been trying to solve

00:01:11.554 --> 00:01:13.560
since the invention of film.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.070
Take this shot of 1909's
"Princess Nicotine."

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.160
The tiny fairy next to
this normal-sized human

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.577
was actually standing on a
platform far behind the table.

00:01:24.577 --> 00:01:27.120
This is known as forced perspective,

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.740
in which one performer in frame

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.870
will place themselves closer to the camera

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.880
and the other farther away.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.880
Forced perspective requires
a sharp depth of field

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.950
so both the foreground and background

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.960
appear equally in focus.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.300
And that means a lot of light.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.640
Visual effects artist Peter Ellenshaw

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.747
said that when working on 1959's

00:01:46.747 --> 00:01:48.840
"Darby O'Gill and the Little People,"

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.005
the production used so many lights

00:01:51.005 --> 00:01:52.680
for the forced-perspective shots

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.510
that it blew out the circuit breakers

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.760
at a nearby power station.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:57.968
Forced perspective works

00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:00.000
when the characters are separated.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.320
When they have to interact,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.420
things get more complicated.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.670
This was an issue Peter
Jackson encountered

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:06.648
when he started shooting

00:02:06.648 --> 00:02:08.970
"The Lord of the Rings:
The Fellowship of the Ring"

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.610
with wizards, dwarves, and hobbits.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.793
To expand how scale could
be used in these movies,

00:02:14.793 --> 00:02:17.520
the crew had to manipulate
their sets and props

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.280
while filming at just the right angle.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.170
Here you can see Gandalf and Frodo

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.760
sitting side by side.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.740
All right, then, keep your secrets.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.590
Narrator: But on set,
Elijah Wood was sitting

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.853
on a shorter stool pushed farther back,

00:02:29.853 --> 00:02:33.150
while Ian McKellen sat right
in front of the camera.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.590
These sorts of raised sets

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.780
are a common forced-perspective technique

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.660
that you can also see
two years later in "Elf."

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.030
And forced perspective
also needs to be paired

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.950
with props that are made to scale.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.266
In this shot, Frodo and
Gandalf were actually

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.930
sitting at a table that
had been split apart.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.660
On one side were
normal-sized props for Frodo.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.060
And for Gandalf, a small-scale table

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.610
with a mug that was two-thirds the size.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.160
The reason these
forced-prospective shots work

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.050
is that the camera stays still.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.840
Most of the camera moves
were quite locked off.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.632
Narrator: Once the camera
moves, the illusion is ruined.

00:03:06.632 --> 00:03:07.920
Eric: It screwed with
your head a little bit

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:08.753
when you were on set,

00:03:08.753 --> 00:03:10.200
because when you watched it from the side,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.570
it didn't look right at all.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.304
Narrator: To accomplish this
moving forced-perspective shot,

00:03:15.304 --> 00:03:17.580
the crew placed the table
on a moving platform

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.750
and programmed the camera

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.850
to move in the opposite direction.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.950
They programmed the
camera's moves in advance,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.870
making them easy to replicate.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.610
That way, in scenes like this one,

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.410
they could shoot the actors separately

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.790
in the exact same way

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.500
and seamlessly combine them.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.450
The shorter Bilbo could walk in front of

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.310
the much taller Gandalf,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.690
and it could all be combined

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.180
with the two versions of Bilbo's house:

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.380
one hobbit-sized

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.063
and one made 33% smaller for Gandalf.

00:03:44.063 --> 00:03:45.180
Oh!

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.590
Narrator: And these shots could consist

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.510
of more than two characters at once.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.569
Nine companions.

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:52.958
Narrator: To get all
four hobbits, two humans,

00:03:52.958 --> 00:03:56.405
a dwarf, an elf, and a
wizard into the same shot,

00:03:56.405 --> 00:03:59.460
the crew filmed scale
composites of each actor

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.960
against a blue screen

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.850
with the proper framing and camera angle

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:04.200
for the characters' heights.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.690
The images were then composited together.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.450
This technology continued
to be perfected and used

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.246
to expand the "Lord of
the Rings" universe.

00:04:12.246 --> 00:04:14.947
But it hit a snag as
Wētā worked on "Avatar"

00:04:14.947 --> 00:04:17.490
and "The Hobbit: An Unexpected Journey,"

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.320
two movies shot in 3D.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.700
Eric: A lot of the cheats

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.140
we were able to get away with before,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.270
we can't get away with as much now.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.890
So instead of shooting with one camera,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.810
you're shooting with side-by-side cameras,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.550
so you get proper 3D.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:32.075
The false perspective really
falls apart quite quickly,

00:04:32.075 --> 00:04:34.230
because you understand
the depth of the shots.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.420
The motion control really kicked into gear

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.265
for the "Hobbit" trilogy.

00:04:38.265 --> 00:04:40.898
Narrator: At this point, the
cameras were much more precise

00:04:40.898 --> 00:04:43.511
and reliable than they were
in the original trilogy.

00:04:43.511 --> 00:04:44.910
Like during this sequence.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.200
Eric: It got the results you needed.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.060
You were able to do
camera moves you wanted,

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.410
you were able to move around,

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.020
do the different things you
had to do to get the shots.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.550
Narrator: But it can pose a challenge

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:56.700
when the actors are
playing non-CG characters.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.580
Because the actors were
frequently shot in different takes

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.350
to account for the differing heights,

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:04.200
they weren't able to directly
interact with one another.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.940
It wasn't very actor-friendly.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.460
Narrator: Size manipulation
is a lot easier

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.350
if the character is fully CG,

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.060
like the giant in "The BFG,"

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:13.860
played by Mark Rylance.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.019
Mark could be on set
with actor Ruby Barnhill,

00:05:17.019 --> 00:05:20.190
as long as he acted above
her on this platform.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:24.273
But even this movie innovated
new riffs on past techniques.

00:05:24.273 --> 00:05:27.812
For example, Ruby was
surrounded by oversized props

00:05:27.812 --> 00:05:30.363
when her character was
in the giant's house.

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:32.823
But unlike past large-scale props,

00:05:32.823 --> 00:05:34.560
some of them were blue.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.630
That way, the animators
could add movements

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.313
to this giant-sized squash.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.185
Some other tricks to
maintain proper eyeline

00:05:42.185 --> 00:05:45.780
and sense of scale are
low tech but effective.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:46.950
In the "Avengers" films,

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:50.070
Josh Brolin plays the 8-foot-tall Thanos.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.217
The mo-cap sensors he wore were so good

00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:54.670
at picking up subtle
expressions and movements

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.490
that his real height didn't matter

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.982
until he was interacting
directly with other characters.

00:05:59.982 --> 00:06:02.310
So to fill out the
height gap in this shot,

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.500
Josh wore a cutout on top of his head

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.625
so Robert Downey Jr. knew
exactly where to look.

00:06:07.625 --> 00:06:10.470
You can also see the 5'4" Tatiana Maslany

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.880
wearing a cutout here

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.670
to look like she's the 6'7" She-Hulk.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:16.020
Just like in "The BFG,"

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:19.050
she stood on an elevated
platform in these shots

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.420
and occasionally sat on a cushion.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.279
But eyeline isn't the only
issue you need to resolve

00:06:24.279 --> 00:06:26.370
when making a performer taller.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:27.600
In this walking shot,

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.210
She-Hulk isn't standing next to anybody,

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:32.430
but Wētā found another issue.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.740
Tatiana's real stride didn't match up

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.110
with She-Hulk's much longer legs.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.150
Where Tatiana would take 10 steps,

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:40.980
She-Hulk would take seven.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:44.555
That's where retargeting, or
character mapping, comes in.

00:06:44.555 --> 00:06:46.050
This is when the VFX artists

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.940
take all the movements and expressions

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.680
from the performance-capture cameras

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.200
and apply them to the animated character.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.270
When the character is a different size,

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.511
Wētā's software Nuance
needs to fill in the gaps,

00:06:57.511 --> 00:07:00.510
like in translating actor
Sam Worthington's posture

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.880
over his Na'vi body in "Avatar"

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.070
or turning Andy Serkis into Caesar

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:06.840
in the "Planet of the Apes" trilogy.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.603
If he's quadrupedal, you want to make sure

00:07:08.603 --> 00:07:09.960
that what is absolute here

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.120
from his performance is his two feet

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.550
and the markers on the
end of the arm extension.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:17.010
Narrator: The crew worked
with actor Malia Arrayah

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:19.290
to retarget She-Hulk's stride.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.807
Malia could serve as a more
height-appropriate stand-in.

00:07:22.807 --> 00:07:24.660
But this isn't a new practice.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:26.768
In the past, stand-ins were used in shots

00:07:26.768 --> 00:07:28.560
where you couldn't see their face.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.810
By having Malia's movements
recorded on a mo-cap stage,

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:33.930
VFX artists could map her stride,

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.390
along with Tatiana's facial capture,

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:37.530
onto She-Hulk.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:40.470
Everything about you is just working.

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:41.303
Thank you!

00:07:41.303 --> 00:07:43.628
Narrator: The better CG
and camera tech has gotten,

00:07:43.628 --> 00:07:46.350
the more seamless these
scale shots can be.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.120
For all of the technological breakthroughs

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.710
in the original "Avatar,"

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.330
there are actually very few shots

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.090
of humans and Na'vi characters together,

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:57.032
as the process at the time
was much less precise.

00:07:57.032 --> 00:07:58.488
Take it easy. Don't get ahead of yourself.

00:07:58.488 --> 00:08:00.362
Narrator: For instance,
the shot of Neytiri

00:08:00.362 --> 00:08:02.924
holding Jake Sully's human body

00:08:02.924 --> 00:08:05.370
was achieved by two green-suit performers

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:07.620
approximating Na'vi size.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.150
But that new eyeline system

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.190
meant there could be many more shots

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.260
of humans and Na'vi together.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.900
It consisted of a monitor
playing an actor's

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.090
previously recorded performance.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:19.350
That monitor was attached

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.930
to overhead cables at four points.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.453
So whenever Scoresby had
a scene with Quaritch,

00:08:24.453 --> 00:08:27.278
the monitor would play back
Stephen Lang's performance

00:08:27.278 --> 00:08:28.470
at the proper height.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.787
It got rotations, it got height,

00:08:30.787 --> 00:08:32.760
so as he moved around, it knew exactly

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:34.170
where his head was supposed to be,

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:35.610
his eyes were supposed to be,

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:36.900
and it moved around set.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.550
Narrator: Take this shot,
where Quaritch walks alongside

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:41.520
two human-sized characters.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.270
We had a live-action
character, Kevin Dorman,

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:45.870
actually walking beside them.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.610
Kevin Dorman is the easy one, right?

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:49.290
He's just live-action character

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.000
walking on a construction set.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.630
But he has to match the speed of Slang,

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.190
which was performance capture.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:58.050
So we take the performance capture,

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.933
and we put it on the cable
cam, the eyeline system,

00:09:00.933 --> 00:09:04.030
and we actually ran that
down the construction set,

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:07.261
and Kevin Dorman was able
to walk along with him,

00:09:07.261 --> 00:09:08.400
and he had an eyeline,

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.350
so he knew the speed he
was supposed to walk.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:11.670
Narrator: But it wasn't just the actors

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.680
who needed precise references.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.600
Real-time depth compositing

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:18.630
allowed James Cameron to
see the CG performances

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.610
as the footage was being captured.

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.080
By seeing a rough version

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.480
of what the finalized
CG shot would look like,

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.208
the director could better set up shots

00:09:26.208 --> 00:09:28.590
of CG and live-action characters

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:30.630
without worrying about
a single camera move

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.190
ruining the illusion.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:36.032
Wētā also used real-time
compositing on the first "Avatar,"

00:09:36.032 --> 00:09:39.180
but the traditional combining
of CG and live-action footage

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.340
is typically done in layers.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:44.140
Eric: Back in the day, you
sort of had an A over B,

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:47.022
which meant you had either
the live action or the CG,

00:09:47.022 --> 00:09:49.260
and it was one over the other.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.210
Narrator: The new system
built for "The Way of Water"

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:54.976
looked at each shot's elements
in pixels instead of layers.

00:09:54.976 --> 00:09:58.052
Eric: We wanted to place
the depth of each pixel

00:09:58.052 --> 00:09:59.070
as we were shooting,

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.290
so that instead of doing A over B,

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.570
we could take each point on space and say,

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:06.570
you are either in front or
behind the other character.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.550
Narrator: And that helps
achieve the correct scale.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:10.830
Eric: You could actually
see how big someone was,

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:13.905
because otherwise you're just
getting that fake perspective,

00:10:13.905 --> 00:10:14.970
even inadvertently,

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.912
because a character is always
over the top of one another.

00:10:17.912 --> 00:10:19.710
Narrator: Thanks to all
of Wētā's innovations,

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.020
making a character look bigger or smaller

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:24.000
is no longer a guessing game.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.460
Those perspectives can be less forced.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.340
But even in the completely CG Pandora,

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.880
some of the most convincing
scale illusions are tangible,

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.438
like when Quaritch holds up
his own human-sized skull.

00:10:36.438 --> 00:10:39.300
On set there was an actual prop skull,

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.820
but scaled down from human size.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.580
That way, the actor's
hand looked Na'vi-sized.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:45.480
Eric: When he crushes it,

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:47.550
it feels like a normal-sized skull,

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:48.792
because you never think about

00:10:48.792 --> 00:10:51.367
his hand being a small, normal-sized hand.

00:10:51.367 --> 00:10:53.730
You just assume it's the
Na'vi's hand crushing it,

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:56.790
because you go from a
shot of Slang to a hand,

00:10:56.790 --> 00:11:00.098
and your brain just fills
it in as just a big hand

00:11:00.098 --> 00:11:02.315
instead of a painted smaller hand.

